WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.398
Authentication and session management are central to Node.

00:00:03.398 --> 00:00:08.346
js application security because many services handle users, API clients, service accounts,

00:00:08.346 --> 00:00:09.995
administrators, and automated jobs.

00:00:09.995 --> 00:00:13.705
Password handling should rely on appropriate password hashing, policy,

00:00:13.705 --> 00:00:17.004
and reset flows rather than custom storage ideas.

00:00:17.004 --> 00:00:23.188
Multi-factor authentication can reduce account takeover risk when designed and operated with clear recovery processes.

00:00:23.188 --> 00:00:28.547
Session cookies and JSON Web Tokens solve different problems and create different risks.

00:00:28.547 --> 00:00:33.495
Cookies can be protected with attributes that reduce exposure in browser contexts,

00:00:33.495 --> 00:00:38.030
but they need CSRF consideration when automatically sent by the browser.

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:43.802
JWTs can be useful for stateless or distributed designs, but teams must manage signing,

00:00:43.802 --> 00:00:47.512
validation, audience, issuer, expiration, rotation, and revocation expectations carefully.

00:00:47.512 --> 00:00:48.749
Token lifetime matters.

00:00:48.749 --> 00:00:51.635
Long-lived access tokens increase exposure when leaked.

00:00:51.635 --> 00:00:55.758
Refresh tokens need stronger protection because they can extend access.

00:00:55.758 --> 00:00:59.881
API keys should be scoped, rotated, monitored, and stored securely.

00:00:59.881 --> 00:01:04.828
The server should validate every request based on current policy rather than

00:01:04.828 --> 00:01:09.363
trusting that possession of a token automatically answers every authorization question.

00:01:09.363 --> 00:01:14.723
Secrets should not be hard-coded in source code or casually placed in logs,

00:01:14.723 --> 00:01:18.433
build output, container images, client bundles, or CI/CD configuration.

00:01:18.433 --> 00:01:22.144
Environment variables can be part of a secret strategy,

00:01:22.144 --> 00:01:26.267
but they are not a complete security program by themselves.

00:01:26.267 --> 00:01:29.153
Secret managers, restricted access, rotation, audit logging,

00:01:29.153 --> 00:01:34.100
and safe deployment practices help keep secrets from becoming permanent hidden liabilities.
